-### Vertex Shader Logic (GLSL-like)
-
-```glsl
-#version 460
-
-layout(location = 0) in vec3 aLocalPosition;
-layout(location = 1) in vec3 aNormal;
-
-layout(binding = 0) buffer VoxelData {
-    uvec2 data[];
-};
-
-uniform mat4 view_projection_matrix;
-uniform vec3 camera_origin;
-
-out vec3 color;
-out vec3 clip_pos;
-out vec3 world_pos;
-
-void main() {
-    // Determine instance index
-    // Note: Source [S2, unofficial] uses gl_VertexID >> 3, implying 8 vertices per instance.
-    // If using index buffer with 18 indices, gl_VertexID goes 0..17.
-    // The source code snippet uses gl_VertexID & 0x1 etc., which implies gl_VertexID < 8.
-    // This suggests the index buffer might not be used for vertex selection in the shader logic provided,
-    // or the shader logic is for a specific vertex ordering.
-    // We follow the source code snippet for the transformation logic.
-    
-    uint instance = gl_VertexID >> 3; // Assuming 8 vertices per instance for this logic
-    
-    // Fetch data
-    uvec2 voxel_data = data[instance];
-    uint packed_pos = voxel_data.x;
-    uint packed_mat = voxel_data.y;
-    
-    // Unpack position
-    uint x = packed_pos & 0x7FF;
-    uint y = (packed_pos >> 11) & 0x3FF;
-    uint z = (packed_pos >> 21) & 0x7FF;
-    
-    // Convert to float world position (assuming unit voxels for simplicity, or scale)
-    vec3 world_position = vec3(float(x), float(y), float(z)) * 0.05; // Scale factor from source
-    
-    // Unpack albedo
-    uint u_red = packed_mat & uint(31);
-    uint u_green = (packed_mat & uint(2016)) >> uint(5);
-    uint u_blue = (packed_mat & uint(63488)) >> uint(11);
-    vec3 albedo = vec3(float(u_red)/32.0, float(u_green)/64.0, float(u_blue)/32.0);
-    
-    // Determine face orientation
-    vec3 local_camera_pos = camera_origin - world_position;
-    
-    // Use gl_VertexID to determine local vertex index (0-7)
-    // This assumes gl_VertexID is 0-7. If index buffer is used, this logic needs adjustment.
-    // Source [S2, unofficial] code:
-    uvec3 xyz = uvec3(gl_VertexID & 0x1, (gl_VertexID & 0x2) >> 1, (gl_VertexID & 0x4) >> 2);
-    
-    // Flip coordinates based on camera position to cull back faces
-    if (local_camera_pos.x > 0.0) {
-        xyz.x = 1 - xyz.x;
-    }
-    if (local_camera_pos.y > 0.0) {
-        xyz.y = 1 - xyz.y;
-    }
-    if (local_camera_pos.z > 0.0) {
-        xyz.z = 1 - xyz.z;
-    }
-    
-    vec3 uvw = vec3(xyz);
-    vec4 position = vec4(uvw * vec3(2.0) - vec3(1.0), 1.0);
-    
-    // Transform
-    position = view_projection_matrix * vec4(position.xyz * 0.05 + world_position, 1.0);
-    
-    clip_pos = position.xyz;
-    world_pos = world_position;
-    gl_Position = position;
-    color = albedo;
-}
-```
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **VAO Availability:** VAOs are not universally available via pure GLES30 on Android; explicit VBO/EBO binding is performed every frame, though overhead is negligible due to the single draw call [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Zoom Clamping:** Zoom factor is clamped to `[MIN_ZOOM, MAX_ZOOM]` to prevent extreme scaling artifacts [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Rotation Direction:** Rotation direction depends on drag direction across screen halves (right/left), handled naturally by the sign of `dx` [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Precision:** Bit-packing coordinates into `uvec2` limits the maximum grid size. X and Z are limited to 2048 (11 bits), Y to 1024 (10 bits) [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Procedural Instancing for Voxel Blocks
-Generates geometry on the fly without large index buffers, suitable for sparse data. [Link](1637-procedural-instancing-for-voxel-blocks.md)
+*   **What it is:** Renders voxel blocks by generating geometry procedurally in the vertex shader, often using point sprites or instanced quads.
+*   **Difference:** Uses point sprites or quads instead of explicit cube faces. Simpler geometry but less precise face culling.
+*   **When to choose:** When hardware supports efficient point sprite rasterization and exact cube geometry is not critical.
+*   **Link:** [Procedural Instancing for Voxel Blocks](1637-procedural-instancing-for-voxel-blocks.md)
 
 ### Instanced Voxel Renderer
-Uses point sprites and ray-marching in the fragment shader, faster on dedicated GPUs but less geometrically precise. [Link](4102-instanced-voxel-renderer.md)
+*   **What it is:** A general term for rendering voxels using instancing techniques, often involving complex culling and LOD.
+*   **Difference:** May use various primitive types (points, quads, cubes) and culling strategies.
+*   **When to choose:** When a more general or optimized rendering pipeline is needed.
+*   **Link:** [Instanced Voxel Renderer](4102-instanced-voxel-renderer.md)
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** O(1) draw call overhead plus O(N) vertex processing for N instances [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** O(N) for SSBO and index buffer storage [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** On dedicated graphics cards, `MODE_CUBE` is approximately twice as slow as `MODE_QUAD` (point sprite/ray-marched) [S2, unofficial]. On Intel Integrated graphics, performance is similar [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Exact cube geometry, explicit face culling, standard rasterization lighting.
-*   **Limitations:** Higher memory bandwidth usage due to index buffer and SSBO; slower on dedicated GPUs compared to point-sprite methods.
-*   **When to Use:** When geometric precision and standard lighting are required, and hardware supports efficient instancing.
-*   **When Not to Use:** When maximum performance on dedicated GPUs is critical and approximate geometry is acceptable.
